%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A26CFBED] ## Background
%[text] There are two broad categories of variables. Qualitative variables, which are nonnumeric, are often names, categories, or qualities such as gender or color. By contrast, quantitative variables are numerical and are the result of a count or measurement like time, distance, or cost. In this live script we will be considering quantitative variables.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0FEFE610] ## Histogram
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_53C4B7DC] A histogram is like a bar chart for quantitative variables. Classes, which are mutually exclusive (non-overlapping) and collectively exhaustive (all values are used) groupings of the data, are indicated along the x-axis. Class frequencies are described along the y-axis. Inasmuch, taller bars indicate classes with greater frequencies. Importantly, histograms differ from bar charts in that there is no gap between classes when the variable is continuous, as is most often the case.
%[text] Here, we create a set of values from the standard normal distribution\*.\*
rng(1)              % set the seed for the random number generator to reuse the same data
x1 = randn(30,1);   % create a column vector of standard normal values
%[text] Next, we observe the the effects of changing the mean and standard deviation on the position and shape of the histogram.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:618e) ***Task***: Click the ***Run*** *button to plot the histogram for normal distribution with mean,* *`mu`* *and standard deviation,* *`sd`**.*
reset = true;                   % uncheck this box to plot the next histogram over the existing plot %[control:checkbox:847d]{"position":[9,13]}
mu =50; % choose a value for the mean %[control:slider:4d1d]{"position":[5,7]}
sd = 3; % choose a value for the sd %[control:slider:732e]{"position":[6,7]}
  %[control:button:307f]{"position":[1,2]}

x2 = sd.*x1 + mu;               % scale by the choosen sd and shift by the mean

% Reset figure if check box is enabled
figure(1000)                    
if reset
    clf('reset')
end
ShowHistogram(mu,sd,x2);        % plot the histogram using the helper function provided
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4e70) ***Try***
%[text] 1. *Uncheck the* *`reset`* *check box.*
%[text] 2. *Use the sliders to set* *`mu`* *to 30 and* *`sd`* *to 6. Click the* ***Run*** *button to plot the new histogram over the existing plot.*
%[text] 3. *Use the sliders to set* *`mu`* *to 20 and* *`sd`* *to 1. Click the* ***Run*** *button to plot the new histogram over the existing plot.*
%[text] 4. *Check the* *`reset`* *check box to plot this next histogram on a cleared figure. Use the sliders to set* *`mu`* *to 50 and* *`sd`* *to 3. Click the* ***Run*** *button.*
%[text] 5. *Uncheck the* *`reset`* *check box to continue plotting more histograms over the new plot.* \
%[text] ![](text:image:91ac) **Reflect**
%[text] - Where does the mean value appear in the histogram?
%[text] - What does the standard deviation value represent in the histogram? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_27C3270F] ## Measures of Center
%[text] To describe the distribution, or collection of values of a quantitative variable, we are often interested in measures of center. These include the average or arithmetic mean, the median, and the mode (usually used to describe qualitative variables). The mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of values and is subject to influence by extreme values, or outliers. This forces the mean toward the direction of extreme.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mu =\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^N x\_i }{N}${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}     mu, the population mean for a population of size N
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\bar{x} =\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^n x\_i }{n}${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}     x bar, the sample mean for a sample of size n        
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_61B93A16] The median, sometimes denoted as x tilde, $\\tilde{x}${"editStyle":"visual"}, is considered robust in that it is not influenced by outliers and is the center of the distribution. With half of the observations below the median and half of the observations above the median it is also called the 50th percentile. 
%[text] ***Task***: Click the ***Run*** *button to plot the measures of center over a histogram.*
distribution = 30-random('chisquare',2,1000,1); %[control:dropdown:6df3]{"position":[16,47]}
distMean = mean(distribution);         % find the mean
distMed = median(distribution);        % find the median
  %[control:button:5998]{"position":[1,2]}

% Plot the histogram
figure(1001)                          % assign the figure number 1001
ShowMeanMed(distribution,distMean,distMed);
%[text] ![](text:image:8074) ***Try***
%[text] - *Use the dropdown to select different types of distributions and observe their means and medians. Switching back to the same type of distribution will always produce a different set of randomly generated data.* \
%[text] ![](text:image:1bc6) **Reflect**
%[text] - For a skewed distribution, which of the two measures seems to provide a better representation of center? Why?
%[text] - Can you think of other ways of measuring the central tendency of data? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_28634D6F] ## Measures of Spread
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_15C45D16] Another method of describing a distribution is its variation. The amount by which a particular value differs from another is known as a deviation. These deviations, or differences, are used to describe spread. The most basic measure of spread or dispersion is the range, which is the maximum value minus the minimum value.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\mathrm{range}=\\mathrm{maximum}\\;\\mathrm{value}-\\mathrm{minimum}\\;\\mathrm{value}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] This is simply the width of the distribution along the number line. A short-coming of the range is that it does not consider every value in the distribution—only the largest and smallest. Another description of spread is the variance, which does not have this limitation. The variance is the average squared deviation from the mean. With regard to the population, the variance is found using the following formula.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\sigma^2 =\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^N {\\left(x\_i -\\mu \\right)}^2 }{N}${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}     sigma squared, the population variance
%[text] Here the squaring allows both positive and negative deviations to have the same influence on the variance. Since all real numbers squared are nonnegative, the magnitude or size of the squared deviation is similar to absolute value. The units of the variance are squared, so, for example, if x were measured in seconds, the variance would be in seconds squared. It is natural, therefore, to find the square root of the variance. This is known as the standard deviation, sigma, and can be described by the average deviation from the mean.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\sigma =\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 }=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^N {\\left(x\_i -\\mu \\right)}^2 }{N}}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}     sigma, the population standard deviation
%[text] If we are only considering a subset of the population, analogous formulas exist for the sample variance and sample standard deviation. In these sample statistics, the denominator, $n-1${"editStyle":"visual"}, is used in place of $n${"editStyle":"visual"}, as it provides a better estimate for the corresponding population parameters. Also notice the use of Latin symbols for sample statistics, while Greek symbols are used for population parameters. This convention is common throughout the discipline.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $s^2 =\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^n {\\left(x\_i -\\bar{x} \\right)}^2 }{n-1}${"editStyle":"visual"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"}     s squared, the sample variance
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $s=\\sqrt{s^2 }=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum\_{i=1}^n {\\left(x\_i -\\bar{x} \\right)}^2 }{n-1}}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}     s, the sample standard deviation
%[text] 
%[text] Here, we create a matrix containing two columns containing 30 values from a standard normal distribution, which has a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. 
%[text] ![](text:image:62fc) ***Task***: Click ***Run*** *to run this section. Observe the variables created.*
variable1 = 1*randn(30,1); %[control:editfield:16c4]{"position":[13,14]} %[text:anchor:M_F2E7F06D]
variable2 = 1*randn(30,1); %[control:editfield:7161]{"position":[13,14]}
y = [variable1,variable2] 
  %[control:button:82a1]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] The functions [std](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.std.html) and [var](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.var.html) are used to calculate standard deviation and variance respectively, for each column of `y`. Their second input specifies the weighing scheme. Set the second input to **`0`** to get the <u>sample</u> statistic and set it to **`1`** to get the <u>population</u> statistic (as defined above).
samSD = std(y,0);
popSD = std(y,1);
samVar = var(y,0);
popVar = var(y,1);

% Build a table containing the sample and population measures of spread for the variables in y.
rows = ["variable 1","variable 2"];
cols = ["sample sd","pop sd","sample var","pop var"];
T = table(samSD',popSD',samVar',popVar','RowNames',rows,'VariableNames',cols) 
%[text] ![](text:image:5f81) ***Try***
%[text] 1. *To scale the variables, change the values in the* [edit fields](internal:M_F2E7F06D) *to* *`2`* *and* *`5`* *respectively. Run the section and observe values in the table.*
%[text] 2. *Change the scale factor of* *`variable1`* *to 10. Run the section and observe values in the table.*
%[text] 3. *Change the scale factor of* *`variable1`* *to 0.5. Run the section and observe values in the table.* \
%[text] ![](text:image:8755) **Reflect**
%[text] - How does scaling differently affect the standard deviation and variance measures of the variables?
%[text] - Do you notice a consistent relationship between the corresponding sample and population statistics of every variable? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_05185411] ## Measures of Position
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1827EF96] In general, measures of position are quantiles—values that divide a set into equal parts. Common measures of position are quartiles, deciles, and percentiles. Respectively, these refer to dividing the data into fourths, tenths, and hundredths. So, for example, the 2nd quartile is the 5th decile and the 50th percentile, that is, $Q\_2 =D\_5 =P\_{50}${"editStyle":"visual"}, where $\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{50}{100}${"editStyle":"visual"} are equivalent fractions. As stated earlier, the 50th percentile is also called the median. Methods of calculating quantiles vary, but a common description for the percentile follows.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $L\_P =\\left(n+1\\right)\\frac{P}{100}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text]{"align":"center"}      L, the location, or rank, of the Pth percentile
%[text] If L is not a whole number, we interpolate between the two values which surround position L.
%[text] The following function illustrates the concept of a percentile using a random sample from the standard normal distribution. The percentiles shown correspond to the empirical rule, which states that 68% of the observations are within one standard deviation of the mean, and 95% of the observations are within two standard deviations of the mean. Additionally, the 25th (blue), 50th (red), and 75th (blue) percentiles are also shown.
%[text] ***Task:*** *Click* ***Run*** *to run this section. Observe the different percentile lines.*
seed = 1;          % the seed for the random number generator (for reproducibility) %[control:dropdown:5e97]{"position":[8,9]}
n = 100;      % the sample size %[control:slider:18b1]{"position":[5,8]}

% Plot properties
radius = 0.04; % the radius of the points %[control:slider:1ac7]{"position":[10,14]}
spread = 1;  % the spread, or jitter, in the y-direction, to see overlapping points %[control:slider:450d]{"position":[10,11]}
  %[control:button:18a3]{"position":[1,2]}
ShowPercentiles(seed,radius,spread,n)
%[text] ![](text:image:087e) ***Try***
%[text] 1. *Increase the sample size to 300 using the slider to set* *`n`**. Click* ***Run*** *to update the plot.*
%[text] 2. *Set the* *`spread`* *to 3 and* *`radius`* *to* *`0.06`* *to better visualize the points. Click* ***Run*** *to update the plot.*
%[text] 3. *Change the* *`seed`* *to a different value. This seed is used for reproducing randomly generated numbers.*
%[text] 4. *Increase the sample size* *`n`* *to* *`500`* *and run the section.* \
%[text] ![](text:image:08eb) **Reflect**
%[text] - As you increase the number of samples, what happens to the 16th, 50th, and 84th percentiles?
%[text] - How does that relate to the parameters of the standard normal distribution? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B861F260] ## Box plot
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F4BC01D6] Much like a histogram, a box plot is a graphic depiction of a set of values. In particular, a box plot is a visual description of the quartiles. In order to describe four quartiles, five values are required: the minimum, $Q\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}, $Q\_2${"editStyle":"visual"}, $Q\_3${"editStyle":"visual"}, and the maximum. These numbers are commonly referred to as a 5-number summary. The distance $Q\_3 -Q\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}, is called the interquartile range, and a box is drawn around it. Values that are more than 1.5 times the interquartile range below $Q\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} or above $Q\_3${"editStyle":"visual"} are considered outliers, that is, they are unusual relative to the entire set of data and are sometimes eliminated from analysis. Lines, or whiskers, extend from the interquartile range to the minimum and maximum values.  
%[text] Here we select values of the seed to generate five different samples of size 30 from a standard normal distribution. In addition to clearly displaying the quartiles and the interquartile range, notice how a box plot reveals skewness by whisker length. Box plots are also helpful for identifying outliers. A histogram is shown for comparison.
%[text] The function [boxplot](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/boxplot.html) is used to create a box plot and the function [histogram](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/matlab.graphics.chart.primitive.histogram.html) is used to plot a histogram.
%[text] ![](text:image:9539) ***Task:*** *Click* ***Run*** *to run this section. Use the slider to increase or decrease the number of samples.*
seed = 1; % the seed number for the random number generator (for reproducibility) %[control:dropdown:014b]{"position":[8,9]}
rng(seed);

% Generate samples for a normally distributed variable
x = randn(100,1); %[control:slider:1ae7]{"position":[11,14]}
  %[control:button:566d]{"position":[1,2]}

figure;
subplot(1,2,1)
boxplot(x,'Orientation','horizontal','Labels',{''})
title('Boxplot')
xlabel('X')

subplot(1,2,2)
histogram(x)
title('Histogram')
xlabel('X')
ylabel('Frequency')
%[text] ![](text:image:5045) ***Try***
%[text] - *Use the dropdown to change the random number generator seed to* *`3`**. Run the section.*
%[text] - *Increase the number of samples to* *`500`**. Run the section.* \
%%
